{
	email ${acme_email}
	# `ask` gates every on-demand issuance (both the bare *.${domain_name}
	# block below and the app./admin. block further down) behind a real
	# tenant-slug lookup, so a made-up subdomain can't burn Let's Encrypt
	# rate limits -- see apps/web's /api/internal/tls-ask route.
	on_demand_tls {
		ask http://web:3000/api/internal/tls-ask
	}
}

# TLS termination lives here now, not in Traefik -- see
# docker-compose.stackit.yml.tftpl (traefik.ports override) and
# traefik.yaml.tftpl for why. Traefik still does all the actual HTTP-layer
# routing (Host/path rules, middlewares, oauth2-proxy forwarding); Caddy
# just terminates TLS and reverse-proxies the decrypted request to it.
#
# on_demand rather than Caddy's normally-eager automatic HTTPS for named
# hosts: on a from-scratch VM (2026-08-26) that eager path silently never
# attempted issuance for these three (no error, no log line -- root cause
# not identified under time pressure during a live outage), while on_demand
# was independently confirmed working in the same instance. The `ask`
# endpoint explicitly allowlists these three fixed hostnames (see
# apps/web's /api/internal/tls-ask route) since they aren't tenant-derived.
saas.${domain_name} {
	tls {
		on_demand
	}
	reverse_proxy traefik:80
}

auth.${domain_name} {
	tls {
		on_demand
	}
	reverse_proxy traefik:80
}

oauthproxy.${domain_name} {
	tls {
		on_demand
	}
	reverse_proxy traefik:80
}

# Per-tenant root hosts, e.g. {slug}.${domain_name} -- Traefik already has
# correct routers for these (dynamic-riogentix-tenants.yaml.tftpl,
# tenant-subdomain-root etc.), but without a Caddy site block matching them,
# Caddy silently serves its own empty default response for any unmatched
# host instead of ever forwarding to Traefik (found 2026-08-22, testing a
# real tenant: DNS and Traefik were both fine, nothing reached Traefik at
# all). on_demand issues a normal Let's Encrypt cert per-hostname on first
# visit, same mechanism as the named hosts above, just deferred to request
# time instead of a fixed name list.
#
*.${domain_name} {
	tls {
		on_demand
	}
	reverse_proxy traefik:80
}

# Two-level tenant subdomains -- a `*.${domain_name}` cert only ever covers
# one wildcard label, so app.{slug}/admin.{slug} need their own on-demand
# coverage. `app.*.${domain_name}` as a site ADDRESS fails Caddy's own
# automatic-HTTPS validation ("subject does not qualify for certificate")
# -- an X.509 wildcard can only be the leftmost label, so a middle-position
# `*` isn't valid there even though on-demand issuance itself doesn't need
# a real wildcard cert. Catch-all :443 + a `host` request matcher (which
# DOES allow a wildcard in any label position, unlike a site address) sidesteps
# that. The `ask` endpoint above (not just the host match) is what keeps
# this from minting a cert for an arbitrary app.<anything>.${domain_name}.
:443 {
	tls {
		on_demand
	}
	@tenant-app-admin host app.*.${domain_name} admin.*.${domain_name}
	handle @tenant-app-admin {
		reverse_proxy traefik:80
	}
	handle {
		respond 404
	}
}
